verdict.evidence:
The cited code matches the finding verbatim and is reachable via the normal, intended unlock flow. No guard, backup, or existsSync check exists.

1) THE OVERWRITE (swarmMemory.ts:1433-1443):
```
function loadOrCreateSalt(): Buffer {
  const p = saltPath()
  if (!p) return newSalt()
  try {
    const b = Buffer.from(fs.readFileSync(p, 'utf8').trim(), 'base64')
    if (b.length === 16) return b
  } catch { /* create below */ }
  const s = newSalt()
  try { fs.writeFileSync(p, s.toString('base64')) } catch { /* best effort */ }
  return s
}
```
ANY throw from readFileSync OR any non-16-byte buffer falls through to newSalt() + fs.writeFileSync(p,...) — overwriting the existing file. There is NO fs.existsSync check to distinguish "absent" from "unreadable/malformed". A successfully-read empty/truncated/corrupt file (length !== 16) triggers the overwrite without needing any exception at all.

2) IT OVERWRITES A SYNCED FILE. swarmMemory.ts:1422 `function saltPath(): string | null { return syncDir ? path.join(syncDir, SALT_FILE) : null }` and :125 `const SALT_FILE = '.termpolis-salt'  // lives in the SYNC folder — shared across devices`. The overwritten salt propagates to every peer.

3) SALT IS CRITICAL KEY MATERIAL. memoryCrypto.ts:28-30 `deriveKey = crypto.scryptSync(passphrase, salt, ...)`. A different salt yields a different key; the original random 16-byte salt is the ONLY copy (saltPath() is referenced only at :1422 and :1434 — no backup anywhere).

4) REACHABLE VIA INTENDED USER ACTION. setSyncPassphrase (swarmMemory.ts:1475) calls loadOrCreateSalt() unconditionally at :1478 BEFORE any validation. Call chain: IPC handler index.ts:1314-1315 `ipcMain.handle('memory:set-sync-passphrase', ... setSyncPassphrase(...))` <- renderer Memory.tsx:78 `memorySetSyncPassphrase(p)` <- the "Unlock" button shown when `sync.locked` (Memory.tsx:285-297: "Encrypted memory found — enter the passphrase to unlock it on this device"). `locked` = lockedShards, set true at swarmMemory.ts:165 `if (dec === null) { lockedShards = true; return }` whenever a synced encrypted line can't be decrypted. So on a second/new device holding peers' ciphertext but no local key, the user is explicitly prompted to type the (correct) passphrase and click Unlock — which runs loadOrCreateSalt against the cloud-synced salt file.

5) OVERWRITE PRECEDES VALIDATION -> BOTH OUTCOMES ARE DESTRUCTIVE (swarmMemory.ts:1478-1487):
```
const key = deriveKey(passphrase, loadOrCreateSalt())   // salt already overwritten here (1441)
const sample = findAnyEncryptedLine()
if (sample && decryptLine(key, sample) === null) {
  throw new Error('Incorrect passphrase for the existing encrypted memory.')  // 1482
}
encKey = key
... writeSecret(p, key...) ; rewriteSelfShard((plain) => encryptLine(key, plain))
```
 - If peer ciphertext is present locally: decrypt fails, user is told "Incorrect passphrase" despite typing the correct one — but the original salt is ALREADY destroyed; once the new salt propagates, all peers' ciphertext (encrypted under the original salt-derived key) is permanently undecryptable.
 - If no peer ciphertext is present locally yet (plausible in the very same partial-sync state that caused the salt read to fail): the `if (sample && ...)` guard is skipped, the wrong key is cached (1484-1486) and this device re-encrypts its shard under it (1487), diverging permanently from the swarm.

6) PREMISE IS REALISTIC. The sync folder is explicitly a cloud provider's (StatusBar.tsx:263 and Memory.tsx:306 name "OneDrive, Google Drive, Dropbox, iCloud, Syncthing"), where dehydrated on-demand placeholders (readFileSync throws), zero-byte transient states (read -> length 0), half-synced truncation (length !== 16), and conflicted-merge corruption (invalid base64) are all well-known conditions.

7) NO TEST GUARDS IT. Only tests/electron/memoryCrypto.test.ts exercises the crypto primitives; loadOrCreateSalt has zero coverage. Ironically that file's line 13 asserts the invariant this bug breaks: "derives the SAME key from the same passphrase + salt (cross-device unlock)".

The proposed fix (create a salt only when fs.existsSync === false; otherwise throw/abort rather than mint-and-write) is correct and necessary.
